WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.160
Color is a powerful cue that convolutional networks
readily exploit for object recognition,

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:11.901
but it becomes a liability when the colors seen at
test time differ from those in training.

00:00:11.981 --> 00:00:16.001
This paper introduces Color Equivariant
Convolutions, or CEConvs,

00:00:16.081 --> 00:00:21.678
a new building block that shares shape features
across the color spectrum while preserving

00:00:21.758 --> 00:00:23.760
discriminative color information.

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:27.419
By hard-wiring parameter sharing over discrete hue shifts,

00:00:27.499 --> 00:00:34.484
CEConvs let networks like ResNets generalize to
underrepresented colors and stay robust to test-time hue shifts,

00:00:34.564 --> 00:00:36.250
without throwing color away.

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:41.314
Convolutional neural networks lean heavily on color
to recognize objects,

00:00:41.394 --> 00:00:45.555
but real-world data rarely contains every color a
class can take.

00:00:45.635 --> 00:00:50.514
When a model trained mostly on red cars sees a blue
one, accuracy collapses.

00:00:50.594 --> 00:00:52.929
The classic remedy, color invariance,

00:00:53.009 --> 00:00:56.126
sidesteps the problem by removing color entirely,

00:00:56.206 --> 00:00:59.193
but that throws away a genuinely useful signal.

00:00:59.273 --> 00:01:05.001
The paper frames the real challenge as keeping
color information while still generalizing

00:01:05.081 --> 00:01:08.590
across colors that were rare or absent during training.

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:15.096
Group equivariant convolutions taught networks to
share parameters across rotations and flips,

00:01:15.176 --> 00:01:19.652
dramatically improving data efficiency for
geometric transformations.

00:01:19.732 --> 00:01:24.142
Yet photometric changes, such as shifts in hue, had
been left aside.

00:01:24.222 --> 00:01:29.820
Studies of trained CNNs show that early layers
learn strongly color-selective neurons,

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:33.517
which suggests color is a natural axis for equivariance.

00:01:33.597 --> 00:01:39.658
This motivates treating a hue shift the same way
prior work treated a rotation: as a symmetry

00:01:39.738 --> 00:01:42.101
the network should respect by design,

00:01:42.181 --> 00:01:45.270
rather than something it must relearn from data.

00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:49.190
The core contribution is the Color Equivariant
Convolution,

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:52.994
a new layer that hard-wires parameter sharing over
hue shifts.

00:01:53.074 --> 00:01:58.394
It shares shape information across the color
spectrum while keeping color in a dedicated

00:01:58.474 --> 00:02:00.541
group dimension of the feature map.

00:02:00.621 --> 00:02:04.223
Because it is formulated in the language of
symmetry groups,

00:02:04.303 --> 00:02:09.315
it slots directly into standard networks like
ResNet with no architectural surgery.

00:02:09.395 --> 00:02:10.788
The authors demonstrate,

00:02:10.868 --> 00:02:14.776
through both controlled toy experiments and
realistic benchmarks,

00:02:14.856 --> 00:02:19.992
that this design improves robustness to color
shifts between training and testing and

00:02:20.072 --> 00:02:22.630
works hand in hand with color augmentation.

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:27.340
A hue shift in HSV space becomes, in RGB space,

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:31.750
a rotation around the gray diagonal from black to white.

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:36.160
The authors formalize this as the group H-n of n
discrete rotations about that diagonal,

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:40.570
a subgroup of all three-dimensional rotations.

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:44.980
A Color Equivariant Convolution correlates the
input with hue-rotated copies of each filter,

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:49.390
producing feature maps that carry an extra
dimension indexing the hue rotation.

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:53.800
In hidden layers,

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:58.210
filters are cyclically permuted across this
dimension so equivariance is preserved throughout the network.

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:02.620
Because the extra dimension multiplies the number
of feature maps,

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:07.030
the authors decompose filters into spatial and
pointwise components and offer hybrid variants

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:11.440
that use color equivariance only in the early,

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:15.850
most color-selective stages, keeping parameter and
compute cost in check.

00:03:16.230 --> 00:03:19.702
The evaluation spans two scales.

00:03:19.782 --> 00:03:23.254
First,

00:03:23.334 --> 00:03:26.805
two synthetic MNIST variants isolate the
phenomenon: a long-tailed ColorMNIST with strong class imbalance,

00:03:26.885 --> 00:03:30.357
and a biased ColorMNIST where each class has a
characteristic hue with tunable spread.

00:03:30.437 --> 00:03:33.909
Then, eight standard image classification benchmarks,

00:03:33.989 --> 00:03:37.461
from CIFAR and STL-10 to Flowers-102, Stanford
Cars, and ImageNet,

00:03:37.541 --> 00:03:41.013
test the method in realistic settings.

00:03:41.093 --> 00:03:44.565
To probe robustness,

00:03:44.645 --> 00:03:48.116
every test image is re-rendered under a gradual hue
shift from minus one-hundred-eighty

00:03:48.196 --> 00:03:51.668
to plus one-hundred-eighty degrees,

00:03:51.748 --> 00:03:55.220
and accuracy is averaged across the full sweep.

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:59.500
The headline finding is robustness without a cost
to clean accuracy.

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:03.480
On the original, unshifted test sets,

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:07.460
color equivariant ResNets perform on par with
vanilla ResNets.

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:11.440
But when the test images are hue-shifted, the gap
opens dramatically.

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:15.420
On Flowers-102,

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:19.400
average accuracy across hue shifts jumps from about
thirteen percent for the baseline

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:23.380
to thirty-three percent for the fully equivariant model,

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:27.360
and similar gains appear on CIFAR-100 and Stanford Cars.

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:31.340
In the controlled long-tailed experiment the
equivariant network reaches ninety-one percent

00:04:31.420 --> 00:04:35.320
against the baseline's seventy-two percent,

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:39.300
with the biggest improvements exactly on the rare
classes that shape sharing is meant to help.

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:44.129
The ablations clarify the design choices.

00:04:44.209 --> 00:04:48.658
Increasing the number of discrete hue rotations
makes the network more robust to test-time hue shifts,

00:04:48.738 --> 00:04:53.187
though it slightly reduces capacity because
channels must shrink to keep parameters fixed.

00:04:53.267 --> 00:04:57.716
Group coset pooling turns out to be the mechanism
that yields hue invariance;

00:04:57.796 --> 00:05:02.245
remove it, and the network behaves like a regular one.

00:05:02.325 --> 00:05:06.774
Finally,

00:05:06.854 --> 00:05:11.303
color equivariance and color-jitter augmentation
are complementary: an equivariant network

00:05:11.383 --> 00:05:15.832
needs a lower intensity of augmentation to reach
the same robustness.

00:05:15.912 --> 00:05:20.361
A color-selectivity analysis further explains when
the method helps,

00:05:20.441 --> 00:05:24.890
showing that datasets with more color-selective
neurons benefit from equivariance up to later stages.

00:05:25.270 --> 00:05:27.440
A few numbers capture the impact.

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:31.325
On Flowers-102 under hue shifts, accuracy nearly triples,

00:05:31.405 --> 00:05:33.916
from thirteen to thirty-three percent.

00:05:33.996 --> 00:05:41.074
On the long-tailed color experiment the equivariant
network gains almost twenty points over the baseline.

00:05:41.154 --> 00:05:48.096
CIFAR-100 improves by fifteen points and Stanford
Cars by roughly thirteen points on hue-shifted tests.

00:05:48.176 --> 00:05:51.572
And all of this comes at a modest compute overhead,

00:05:51.652 --> 00:05:57.639
since the filter decomposition keeps the increase
in operations and parameters to a small

00:05:57.719 --> 00:06:00.230
factor of the number of hue rotations.

00:06:00.610 --> 00:06:06.008
The lasting message is that color deserves the same
equivariance treatment that rotations

00:06:06.088 --> 00:06:08.163
and translations have long enjoyed.

00:06:08.243 --> 00:06:13.025
Instead of choosing between exploiting color and
being robust to color changes,

00:06:13.105 --> 00:06:16.288
Color Equivariant Convolutions let a network do both,

00:06:16.368 --> 00:06:22.197
by sharing shape information across the color
spectrum while keeping color in its own dimension.

00:06:22.277 --> 00:06:26.752
The block plugs into standard architectures, plays
well with augmentation,

00:06:26.832 --> 00:06:30.815
and delivers its largest gains precisely where
color matters most,

00:06:30.895 --> 00:06:34.200
offering a practical route to color-robust recognition.
